    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Josh Naylor** (77.77) and **Brice Turang** (75.06) stand as our elite-tier offensive anchors. **Josh Naylor** benefits immensely from the generous 4.55 xERA of the opposing starter, and his neutral-to-favorable platoon split keeps his floor high in a productive lineup spot. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang**\u2019s efficiency is boosted significantly by a +16% park factor and an 18% advantage against a favorable arm; crucially, his involvement on the basepaths remains our primary engine for Stolen Bases, as his ability to capitalize on the opposing battery makes him a locked-in must-start despite the team\u2019s ongoing pursuit of category volume. **Mickey Moniak** and **Corbin Carroll** also grade out as \"Strong\" starts (64.32 and 62.86, respectively), with both benefiting from slow opposing deliveries that enhance their projected sprint-to-base outcomes.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was passing over **Austin Riley** (54.26). While his baseline projection remains robust, he narrowly missed the cut due to a suboptimal batting order placement (#8) and a lower ceiling compared to **Isaac Paredes**, whose dynamic platoon edge against the opposing arm provides a more favorable path to counting stats. We are monitoring the status of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** closely, as both face high-end arms in Cole and Skenes; while their scores hover near the marginal 50-point threshold, their lineup security keeps them active. With the current roster, we are prioritizing high-OPS environments, though we remain mindful of the risks associated with the high-whiff profiles of starters facing our lineup today. All bench assets\u2014including **Trevor Story**, **Griffin Conine**, and **Jose Caballero**\u2014are being held to preserve our remaining game caps, ensuring we don't bleed efficiency in a crowded Roto leaderboard.",
